
The Adapter design pattern allows two incompatible interfaces to work together by introducing an adapter class.

Story and Problem Explanation:

Imagine you run a service that needs to send notifications to users. Some users want to get emails, others want SMS messages. 
You have two different providers: one for email, one for SMS. But each provider has its own way of sending messages—different method names, 
different parameters.

If you write your code to use the email provider directly, it won’t work for SMS, and vice versa. You’d have to write separate code 
for each provider, making your system complicated and hard to maintain.

The Adapter pattern solves this by introducing a common interface, like NotificationSender, with a method sendNotification(message, recipient). 
You then write an EmailNotificationAdapter and an SmsNotificationAdapter. Each adapter knows how to talk to its provider, 
translating the generic sendNotification call into the specific method the provider expects.

Now, your main code can use NotificationSender for both email and SMS. You can add new notification types in the future 
(like push notifications) just by writing a new adapter, without changing your main logic. This keeps your code clean, flexible, and easy to extend.


1. Define NotificationSender interface with sendNotification(String message, String recipient).
2. Create two provider classes: EmailProvider and SmsProvider, each with their own specific send methods.
3. Implement EmailNotificationAdapter and SmsNotificationAdapter that wrap their respective providers and implement NotificationSender.

    package com.example.notification;

    public interface NotificationSender {
        void sendNotification(String message, String recipient);
    }


    // Java
    package com.example.notification;

    // Simulates a third-party email provider
    public class EmailProvider {

        public void sendEmail(String emailAddress, String content) {
            System.out.println("Email sent to " + emailAddress + ": " + content);
        }
    }

    // Java
    package com.example.notification;

    // Simulates a third-party SMS provider
    public class SmsProvider {

        public void sendSms(String phoneNumber, String text) {
            System.out.println("SMS sent to " + phoneNumber + ": " + text);
        }
    }

    // Java
    package com.example.notification;

    public class EmailNotificationAdapter implements NotificationSender {

        private final EmailProvider emailProvider;

        public EmailNotificationAdapter(EmailProvider emailProvider) {
            this.emailProvider = emailProvider;
        }

        @Override
        public void sendNotification(String message, String recipient) {
            emailProvider.sendEmail(recipient, message);
        }
    }


    // Java
    package com.example.notification;

    public class SmsNotificationAdapter implements NotificationSender {

        private final SmsProvider smsProvider;

        public SmsNotificationAdapter(SmsProvider smsProvider) {
            this.smsProvider = smsProvider;
        }

        @Override
        public void sendNotification(String message, String recipient) {
            smsProvider.sendSms(recipient, message);
        }
    }

    // Java
    package com.example.notification;

    public class Main {

        public static void main(String[] args) {

            NotificationSender emailSender =
                    new EmailNotificationAdapter(new EmailProvider());

            NotificationSender smsSender =
                    new SmsNotificationAdapter(new SmsProvider());

            emailSender.sendNotification("Hello via Email!", "alice@example.com");
            smsSender.sendNotification("Hello via SMS!", "+1234567890");
        }
    }


1. In the email and SMS notification example:
    There are two different providers: one for email, one for SMS. Each has its own method for sending messages.
    Your application wants to use a single interface (e.g., NotificationSender) to send notifications, regardless of the provider.
    You create adapters (EmailNotificationAdapter, SmsNotificationAdapter) that implement NotificationSender and internally call the specific provider's method.

2. This way, your application code interacts only with NotificationSender, and the adapters handle the differences in provider interfaces.


Technical explanation:
1. The Adapter pattern defines a common interface (NotificationSender) for your application.
2. Each adapter class implements this interface and translates the generic method call (sendNotification) into the specific method 
    required by the provider (sendEmail or sendSms).
3. This decouples your application logic from the details of each provider, making it easy to add or switch providers without 
    changing your main code.
4. The pattern is useful when integrating third-party libraries or legacy systems with different APIs into a unified workflow.

